.TH CCLOCK 1
.SH NAME
cclock \- draggable countdown overlay for Wayland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cclock
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cclock
displays a countdown in a layer-shell overlay attached to a screen edge.
Drag it between edges and outputs, click it to pause or resume, and hover over
it to reveal the configured text and close button.
.PP
If no duration is supplied, cclock opens a time picker.  Values supplied with
different duration options are added.  At zero, the overlay changes to the
overtime color and continues to display elapsed time without a leading plus
sign.  Time queries retain the plus sign.
.PP
Control and query options communicate with the running cclock instance.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display all cclock command-line options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Display the cclock version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help-all
Display cclock and generic Qt options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-picker
Open the time picker even when a duration was supplied.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-seconds " " \fINUM\fR
Add NUM seconds to the countdown.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-minutes " " \fINUM\fR
Add NUM minutes to the countdown.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-hours " " \fINUM\fR
Add NUM hours to the countdown.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-text " " \fITEXT\fR
Show TEXT while the overlay is hovered.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-stop
Stop the running instance and exit.  With a query option, print its value
before stopping.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-time-get
Print the running timer value without a leading minute zero and exit.  Overtime
has a leading plus sign.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-text-get
Print the running timer text and exit.  It cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-time-get .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-running-color ", " \-\-color " " \fICOLOR\fR
Set the running blob color.  COLOR must be an opaque Qt color name or hex
value.  The default is #0b0b0d.  The
.B \-\-color
name is retained for compatibility.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-paused-color ", " \-\-pause-color " " \fICOLOR\fR
Set the paused blob color.  The default is #ffd60a.  The
.B \-\-pause-color
name is retained for compatibility.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-overtime-color " " \fICOLOR\fR
Set the overtime blob color.  The default is #d32f2f.  Overtime digits are
dark; while paused, the paused color takes precedence.
.TP
.BR \-\-screen " " \fINAME\fR
Prefer output NAME.  If it is unavailable, cclock uses an available output and
returns to NAME when it reconnects.
.TP
.BR \-\-oled " " \fIOUTPUT\fR
Enable OLED protection for one output.  Use
.B all
for every output or
.B none
to disable it.  Protection applies only while the blob is on the selected
output.  It moves the blob continuously by one collapsed blob size, reduces
its non-hover opacity, and changes running idle overtime to red digits on
black.  Paused digits remain visible.
.TP
.B \-\-notify
Send a desktop notification when this instance is stopped from the overlay or
through a control command.
.TP
.B \-\-pause
Pause the running instance.
.TP
.B \-\-resume
Resume the running instance.
.TP
.B \-\-toggle
Toggle the running instance between paused and running.
.TP
.B \-\-focus
Enter focus mode on the running timer.  This works independently of
.BR keyboard.motion .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cclock/config.toml
User settings.  If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, the path is normally
.IR ~/.config/cclock/config.toml .
The file is created with defaults on first use.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/cclock/cclock.conf
Saved edge, output, position, and last picker value.  If
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset, the path is normally
.IR ~/.local/share/cclock/cclock.conf .
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cclock.sock
Local socket used to control the running instance.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The TOML keys are
.BR colors.running ,
.BR colors.paused ,
.BR colors.overtime ,
.BR oled.output ,
.BR oled.interval ,
.BR oled.timeout ,
.BR keyboard.motion ,
.BR keyboard.step ,
.BR commands.before_start ,
.BR commands.on_zero ,
and
.BR commands.on_stop .
The three command values are run through
.B /bin/sh -c
once at their named lifecycle point.  See the installed project documentation
for a complete example.
.PP
The timer output is persisted in
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/cclock/cclock.conf .
.B \-\-screen
overrides it for one run; there is no timer output key in
.IR config.toml .
.SH KEYBOARD CONTROLS
In focus mode,
.B h
and
.B l
move along horizontal edges, while
.B k
and
.B j
move along vertical edges.  The distance is set by
.BR keyboard.step .
.BR Shift+H/J/K/L
moves the blob to the left, bottom, top, or right edge, respectively.
.B Tab
toggles pause.
.B Ctrl+C
stops the timer.
.B Escape
exits focus mode.  Set
.B keyboard.motion
to true to activate keyboard controls by clicking the blob without showing the
focus outline.  Only
.B \-\-focus
shows that outline.  Dropping the blob after a drag also exits focus mode.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
No running instance accepted a request, or startup failed.
.TP
.B 2
The duration, color, OLED value, or option combination was invalid.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cclock -m 25 -t \(dqWrite tests\(dq
Start a 25-minute countdown with hover text.
.TP
.B cclock -m 1 -s 30 --running-color steelblue --paused-color '#ffd60a'
Start a 90-second countdown with custom colors.
.TP
.B cclock --screen DP-2 --oled DP-2 -m 25
Prefer DP-2 and enable OLED protection on that output.
.TP
.B cclock --stop --time-get
Print the displayed time, then stop the running instance.
